== Problem 3: {0^i 1^j 0^k : j = i + k}
OK   grammar S -> AB, A -> 0A1 | ε, B -> 1B0 | ε generates exactly L, all |w| <= 12
OK   L(A) = {0^n 1^n} and L(B) = {1^n 0^n}, |w| <= 12
OK   examples in L: ε, 01, 10, 0110, 001110; not in L: 0, 1, 0101, 1001
OK   001110 = (0011)(10) with A =>* 0011 and B =>* 10
OK   every string in L has equally many 0s and 1s (|w| <= 14)
OK   fooling set 0*: x = 0^m, y = 0^n (m != n, 0..18), z = 1^m: xz in L, yz not
OK   edge case m = 0: yz = 0^n (n >= 1) is not in L, although it splits as 0^i 1^0 0^k in n+1 ways
OK   trap: the finite set {ε, 0, 00} is a fooling set for L (every pair has a distinguishing suffix)
OK   closure: L ∩ 0*1* = {0^n 1^n}, |w| <= 14
OK   wrong grammar S -> 0S1 | T, T -> 1T0 | ε (nested): generates 0101 not in L, misses 0110 in L
OK   wrong grammar A -> 0A1 | 01, B -> 1B0 | 10: misses ε, 01, 10 and every 0^n1^n
OK   wrong grammar S -> 0S1 | 1S0 | SS | ε (all strings with #0 = #1): generates 1001 and 0101, not in L
